<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="Localize Robot">
  <!--//////////-->
  <BehaviorTree
    ID="Localize Robot"
    _description="Tell the localizer where the robot is: click the robot's location on the map, then the estimate is tightened in place before the robot moves. Run this once after startup, before navigating, and again any time the robot is moved without driving."
    _favorite="true"
  >
    <Control ID="Sequence" name="TopLevelSequence">
      <!--AMCL runs with set_initial_pose: false, because in production a robot does not power on
           at a known pose. This Objective is what gives the filter its first real pose.

           This config runs beluga_amcl, NOT nav2_amcl, and the two differ here in a way that
           matters below. beluga global-initialises on map receipt - it carries both the "about
           initial pose" and the "distributed across the map" initialisation paths - and with
           tf_broadcast true it DOES broadcast map -> odom from that unconverged uniform cloud. So
           the edge exists from startup. It is not a measurement: it is the mean of particles
           spread over every free cell of the map, and on hangar_map that mean sits tens of metres
           from the robot. The edge only starts meaning something once a seed has been consumed.-->
      <Action
        ID="GetPoseFromUser"
        name="Ask the operator where the robot is"
        is_normal="true"
        pose_prompt="Click the robot's current location on the map"
        user_pose="{clicked_pose}"
        view_name="Visualization"
      />
      <!--The UI returns the click in the planning frame - 'world' on this robot, hardcoded in the
           UI's pose-utils.ts - and the seed has to be in 'map'. The route between them is
           world -> odom -> map, and its only non-static link is the map -> odom edge described
           above. Running the click through that edge is exactly what must not happen here: before
           the seed, the edge carries a uniform-cloud mean, so TransformPoseFrame would not fail,
           it would succeed and place the seed tens of metres from where the operator pointed.

           So the click is RELABELLED rather than transformed. mj_world -> map and odom -> world
           are both static identities (robot_drivers_to_persist_sim.launch.py), so world
           coordinates ARE map coordinates whenever map -> odom is identity. The relabel is done by
           its own node, in the open, so that nothing about it is hidden: SeedLocalizationAtPose
           keeps refusing any pose that is not already in 'map', and that guard is worth keeping.

           TWO ASSUMPTIONS, NEITHER OBSERVED IN SIMULATION. Both are the reason this is written out
           rather than buried.

           First, this is exact at cold boot, when map -> odom is identity. It is NOT exact when
           this Objective is re-run after the robot has driven and map -> odom carries a real
           correction: the click is then off by that accumulated correction. For the bumped-robot
           case use "Refine Localization In Place", which seeds from the filter's own estimate and
           never crosses frames at all.

           Second, this is only the right call if the UI does not itself render the click through
           the same map -> odom the transform would have undone. If it does, the round trip would
           have cancelled and TransformPoseFrame was already correct. Settle this against the
           simulator before trusting either path after the robot has moved.-->
      <Action
        ID="ReinterpretPoseFrame"
        name="Read the click as map coordinates without transforming it"
        input_pose="{clicked_pose}"
        frame_id="map"
        output_pose="{initial_pose}"
      />
      <!--Seeding and refining are one operator action, not two. The subtree below seeds from this
           click and then tightens the estimate without moving the base; if the result does not
           explain the live scan it puts this click back, so the worst case here is exactly what a
           click-only localization would have left in the filter.

           The refinement is the SAME subtree "Refine Localization In Place" runs on its own. Keep
           it that way: two copies of a tree that gates a safety-relevant belief is two things to
           keep in step.-->
      <SubTree
        ID="Refine Localization In Place Subtree"
        name="Tighten the estimate without moving"
        seed_pose="{initial_pose}"
        refined_pose="{refined_pose}"
        refined_transform="{refined_transform}"
        seed_to_refined="{seed_to_refined}"
        inlier_fraction="{inlier_fraction}"
        median_residual="{median_residual}"
        beams_used="{beams_used}"
      />
    </Control>
  </BehaviorTree>
  <TreeNodesModel>
    <SubTree ID="Localize Robot">
      <MetadataFields>
        <Metadata runnable="true" />
        <Metadata subcategory="Navigation" />
      </MetadataFields>
    </SubTree>
  </TreeNodesModel>
</root>
